# 1fe102b -- the owner personhood register and the ownership chain it certifies.
# The e802469 hazard in its usual direction, and six times over: deploy_state
# declares SIX more out_of_band files -- owner_personhood_register,
# owner_eligibility_authority, ownership_policy, ownership_selection,
# ownership_shadow and ownership_attribution -- so a public commit pinned earlier
# than this would fail with DECLARED BUT ABSENT FROM DISK on all six.
#
# NONE is promoted into REQUIRED_MIGRATIONS, and for this set the reason is
# sharper than "Railway has not run it". The register must be POPULATED and
# CERTIFIED before owner_eligibility_authority is applied anywhere: an
# authoritative contract over an empty register would correctly refuse every
# identity in the system. Deploy order is owner_personhood_register, then
# owner_eligibility_authority, then ownership_policy, ownership_selection,
# ownership_shadow, ownership_attribution.
#
# Certification is measured over the active role-assignment principals that
# assignable_identity represents, and not over employees. employees is an
# upstream source of employee declarations; a declaration is held against a
# subject uuid while membership is keyed on an address with owner_id as optional
# metadata, so certifying the staff roster would have established nothing about
# the subject the eligibility contract asks about. The reasoning is recorded in
# docs/personhood_domain_contract_gate.md, which arrives in the paired public
# commit.
#
# Also carries two transactional corrections, because a swallowed exception does
# not clear an aborted PostgreSQL transaction. In provision_owner the next
# statement failed with "current transaction is aborted"; in
# grant_employee_owner the next statement was conn.commit(), which PostgreSQL
# converts to a ROLLBACK and reports as success, so a minted owner was discarded
# silently while the caller reported the grant applied. Both are now behind
# savepoints.

